\section{Experiments}
\label{sec:experiments}

We evaluate contrastive trace probes across three reasoning models (R1-8B, R1-32B, QwQ-32B), two signal modalities (hidden states and text), and multiple temporal aggregation strategies. Our experiments address four questions: (1) How much does temporal context improve detection? (2) Does the choice of signal modality matter? (3) Can sequence models detect unsafe reasoning earlier? (4) Do probes transfer across models?

\subsection{Temporal Context as Primary Factor}
\label{sec:temporal_primary}

The most consistent finding across all experimental conditions is that temporal context improves probe precision and reduces FPR, regardless of signal modality. \cref{fig:window_ablation} shows this pattern for R1-8B; precision and FPR gains hold across all three models, though balanced accuracy improvement is model-dependent.

On R1-8B, increasing window size from $W{=}1$ to $W{=}25$ improves balanced accuracy from 77.8\% to 81.0\% (+3.2pp), precision from 69.3\% to 82.2\% (+12.9pp), and reduces step-level FPR from 9.65\% to 4.83\% ($-$4.8pp). QwQ-32B shows a similar monotonic pattern (+3.2pp accuracy, +7.4pp precision, $-$5.9pp FPR from $W{=}1$ to $W{=}25$). R1-32B exhibits a non-monotonic trajectory: accuracy peaks at $W{=}10$ then declines below the $W{=}1$ baseline by $W{=}25$ (71.8\% $\rightarrow$ 70.3\%), though precision and FPR still improve (+1.7pp, $-$5.5pp). Across all three models, FPR consistently decreases with larger windows (\cref{tab:temporal_main}). Larger windows make probes more conservative: fewer traces cross the detection threshold (coverage decreases from 72.1\% to 48.8\% on R1-8B under $K{=}1$ criterion), but those that do are classified with higher confidence.

The coverage-quality trade-off depends on the detection criterion. Under $K{=}1$ (single threshold crossing), larger $W$ reduces coverage because the smoothed probe score crosses the boundary fewer times. Under stricter criteria ($K{=}5$, requiring consecutive crossings), larger $W$ can improve effective coverage by eliminating transient false crossings. We report $K{=}1$ results throughout for interpretability, noting that deployment systems would tune $K$ alongside $W$.

The key observation is that the magnitude of temporal improvement on precision (+1.7 to +12.9pp) and FPR ($-$4.8 to $-$5.9pp) dwarfs the modality gap (0 to 7pp FPR difference in the strongest case). Temporal context is modality-agnostic: whether probes read hidden activations or generated text, observing multiple reasoning steps provides the dominant source of discriminative information for FPR reduction.

\subsection{Hidden States vs. Text: Model-Dependent Effects}
\label{sec:hs_vs_text}

Given the dominance of temporal context, we ask whether signal modality produces a consistent residual effect. \cref{tab:hs_vs_text} reports accuracy, precision, and pre-commitment FPR for HS and text probes across all three models. Note that each model uses its best-performing temporal window ($W{=}15$ for R1-8B, $W{=}10$ for R1-32B, $W{=}5$ for QwQ-32B), so cross-model comparisons reflect model-specific optima rather than a uniform condition.

On R1-8B ($n{=}43$), HS probes Pareto-dominate text probes with strong statistical support: higher precision (69.7\% vs.\ 47.2\%, +22.4pp, $p{<}0.001$), higher accuracy (81.3\% vs.\ 72.9\%, +8.2pp, $p{=}0.005$), and lower FPR (14.8\% vs.\ 22.2\%, $\Delta{=}{-}7.4$pp, $p{=}0.044$). Both precision and accuracy differences survive Bonferroni correction for three model comparisons ($\alpha{=}0.017$); the FPR difference does not. Precision comparisons exclude traces where neither probe produced a positive prediction (10/43 on R1-8B), as precision is undefined in this case; the bootstrap operates on the remaining 33 traces.\footnote{Excluding undefined-precision traces is conservative: these traces represent cases where both probes correctly abstain, and including them would not change the direction of the effect.} This is a genuine dominance result where HS probes improve on every metric simultaneously, with the two strongest effects confirmed at high statistical confidence.

The 32B models present a different picture. On R1-32B, HS probes show directional advantages in precision (+9.4pp, $p{=}0.064$) and accuracy (+6.2pp, $p{=}0.13$), but neither reaches significance. On QwQ-32B, differences are negligible: precision +5.8pp ($p{=}0.35$), accuracy +0.9pp ($p{=}0.83$). The two modalities operate at different precision-FPR tradeoff points rather than one dominating the other. These non-significant results on 32B models are consistent with both genuine model-dependent effects and insufficient statistical power given smaller test sets ($n{\approx}20$).

At the step level, R1-8B reveals an asymmetry in deployment-relevant terms (\cref{fig:model_dependency}). The HS probe achieves 8.8\% step-level FPR compared to 20.5\% for the text probe. For the 32B models, step-level FPR differences are negligible.

The practical implication is that modality choice is model-dependent. On R1-8B, HS probes are clearly preferred. On 32B models, the choice depends on whether the deployment scenario prioritizes precision (favoring HS) or low FPR (favoring text). Per-model validation is necessary rather than blanket modality assumptions.

\subsection{Detection Timing Profiles}
\label{sec:timing}

Beyond aggregate accuracy, we examine when probes first detect unsafe reasoning relative to the commitment point (the step at which the model irreversibly begins generating harmful content).

On R1-8B, the HS temporal probe detects unsafe traces at approximately the commitment point itself (lead $\approx$ 0 steps, low FPR). The text temporal probe detects roughly 10 steps earlier (lead $\approx$ +10), but at the cost of higher FPR. This timing asymmetry reflects the nature of the two signals: text features become discriminative as soon as the model begins articulating a plan, while hidden state features consolidate only when the internal representation fully commits to the unsafe trajectory.

This timing trade-off is specific to R1-8B. On QwQ-32B and R1-32B, both modalities show comparable detection timing with no reliable FPR difference between them. We caution against interpreting the R1-8B pattern as a universal property of the two modalities.

\subsection{GRU Sequence Models}
\label{sec:gru}

Static and windowed probes discard sequential structure. We train Gated Recurrent Unit (GRU) classifiers on the full step sequence to ask whether temporal dependencies beyond a fixed window improve early detection. \cref{tab:gru_results} reports crossing accuracy (the accuracy at which the probe first exceeds chance with sustained performance) and detection lead (steps before the commitment point).

For R1-8B, a single-layer GRU trained on layer 14 hidden states achieves 83.9\% peak accuracy with a crossing point at 72.1\% and a mean lead of +3.5 steps across three seeds. A multi-layer GRU using seven layers reaches 72.9$\pm$4.0\% crossing accuracy with 81.8$\pm$1.0\% peak accuracy, a pre-commitment FPR of 16.5$\pm$4.1\%, and extends the lead to +4.5$\pm$3.0 steps. The worst individual seed achieves a lead of only +0.9 steps, indicating high variance in early detection on this model.

QwQ-32B benefits more from sequence modeling. The single-layer GRU achieves 75.0\% crossing accuracy with a lead of +19.3 steps; the multi-layer variant reaches 84.0\% crossing with +15.9 steps lead. These longer leads suggest that QwQ-32B's hidden states encode planning information well before the commitment point.

R1-32B shows weaker GRU performance overall: single-layer crossing at 30.0\% with +9.1 lead, multi-layer at 23.0\% with +8.8 lead. The low crossing accuracy indicates that R1-32B's safety-relevant signal is more diffuse across layers and harder to capture with a compact sequence model.

All results are reported as mean $\pm$ standard deviation over three random seeds.

\subsection{Control Task: Signal Validation}
\label{sec:control}

A probe that achieves high accuracy on structured labels might simply exploit positional information (e.g., unsafe steps tend to occur later in a trace). Following \cite{hewitt2019control}, we train control probes on randomly permuted labels to measure selectivity: the gap between linguistic probe accuracy and control probe accuracy.

\cref{tab:control} reports selectivity scores: R1-8B 12.5pp (layer 14), QwQ-32B 15.3pp (layer 2), R1-32B 7.5pp (layer 10). All three models exceed the 5pp threshold typically considered meaningful, confirming that probes exploit genuine safety-related structure in the representations rather than positional artifacts alone.

As an additional validation, we compare the HS probe FPR against a position-only baseline that predicts ``unsafe'' based solely on step index. On R1-8B, the probe clearly outperforms the position baseline (FPR 8.1\% vs.\ 15.8\%), confirming genuine safety encoding. On QwQ-32B (layer 2), the position baseline accuracy (70.6\%) slightly exceeds the HS probe accuracy (67.8\%), and on R1-32B (layer 10) selectivity is near the threshold at 7.5pp. These results suggest that 32B model probes at their selected layers encode safety state partially through positional correlations, though selectivity still exceeds the 5pp criterion on all models.

\subsection{Negative Results: Change-Point and Geometric Methods}
\label{sec:negative}

We evaluate two families of methods from the anomaly detection literature that, a priori, seem well-suited to temporal safety monitoring.

\paragraph{Bayesian Online Change-Point Detection (BOCPD).} With hazard rate $\lambda{=}10$, BOCPD applied to probe scores achieves 93\% detection rate but with a mean lead of only +3.5 steps. A simple threshold on the temporal probe score achieves +4.9 steps lead with comparable detection rate. CUSUM (Cumulative Sum) variants perform worse across all configurations. The sequential structure of probe scores does not exhibit the abrupt distributional shifts that change-point methods are designed to detect; instead, the transition is gradual. \cref{tab:negative} reports detailed comparisons.

\paragraph{Mahalanobis Distance (TrajGuard).} We compute Mahalanobis distances of hidden state trajectories from class-conditional distributions. Safe traces (6.66) and pre-commitment jailbreak traces (6.73) produce nearly identical Mahalanobis distances, which further decrease post-commitment (6.64) on R1-8B. The geometric separation between trajectory distributions is negligible in the high-dimensional activation space, rendering distance-based methods ineffective for this task.

These negative results carry practical value: they redirect future work away from off-the-shelf change-point and geometric anomaly detection toward learned temporal classifiers.

\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}
\label{sec:transfer}

We test whether a probe trained on one model's hidden states can detect unsafe reasoning in another model. For each source-target pair, we apply PCA to align the source model's representation space to the target's dimensionality, then evaluate the transferred probe.

All transfer directions fail. The best-performing transfer (QwQ-32B $\rightarrow$ R1-32B) achieves 67.3\% accuracy against the within-model baseline of 65.2\%, a margin within noise. Other directions perform at or below chance.

This result indicates that safety-relevant representations are model-specific. Each model encodes reasoning trajectories in idiosyncratic geometric configurations that do not share a common subspace across architectures. Deployment of contrastive trace probes requires per-model training; a universal probe is not feasible with current methods.

\subsection{Oracle Decomposition}
\label{sec:oracle}

To quantify the remaining performance gap, we construct an oracle detector with access to $K{=}5$ future steps (lookahead). The oracle achieves 100\% detection rate with 76.7\% of detections classified as ``early'' (before the commitment point), and a mean lead of +31 steps.

The oracle achieves +31 steps lead time; our best learned method (GRU) achieves +4.5 steps, representing an information gap of $1 - (4.5/31) = 85.5\%$ when measured as the fraction of oracle lead that remains uncaptured. Hidden states encode the model's current safety state reliably, as evidenced by high probe accuracy, but they poorly predict future trajectory evolution. The majority of early detection potential remains unrealized, suggesting that safety monitoring at inference time faces a fundamental information bottleneck: the model's internal state at step $t$ carries limited predictive power about behavior at step $t{+}k$ for large $k$.

This decomposition provides a concrete upper bound for future work on temporal safety probes and clarifies that the primary challenge is not representation quality but temporal prediction.

